          "collection_date": "1964",
          "description": {
            "comment": "Clostridium taeniosporum is a spore-forming, anaerobic, non-pathogen which is unique. About twelve large, ribbon-like appendages are attached to one spore pole. The appendages are assembled from smaller complexes consisting of a spherical head and a long, thin tail. The spherical heads assemble in long parallel rows to form on surface of the appendages; the tails point away to form the other surface. The appendages are so large that about 50,000 small complexes are required to form each appendage. This organism has great potential in studies of developmental biology, structural biology, industrial uses (e.g., as a surface display host to express epitopes for vaccine use), in clostridial phylogeny and in environmental microbiology. Analysis of its genome will provide the basis for those studies.",
